{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on exactd428679124df099209e7371cc6286d8ddcc07447. Sole primary03923c21f1cb0cf165547705ca38a133cf29ed51 read first/full, all363lines, and original review9a646ac8 reread. Draft2 closes most of the original scope: every:1 removes grindable selection; configuration/stage comparison is explicit; source/reuse eligibility, indexed lookup, fresh-run recovery, default member semantics and source citations are materially improved. Two bounded gaps remain.\n\nF1 — High, Holds lines203–234 (original F2 not fully closed): recording check/configuration/rules revision/extent in a hold is not enough unless recovery matches them. The clearing rule currently says same-or-later commit and, on one commit, \"the later job decides\"; it does not say which check/source/configuration/rules generation that later job must belong to, or whether a changed policy explicitly supersedes a hold. An old-policy result or another check must not clear a newer held failure merely because its commit/job sorts later. Define the recovery identity and admission rule (for example a current-authorized recovery job explicitly bound to the held key/generation); configuration/rules changes require an explicit transition or override, not an implicit successful unrelated result. Use a destination-recorded publication/job order rather than treating Git hashes or unrelated job IDs as a total order. Also name the admission cut: pending post-publication checks may permit later admissions before failure is known, or may hold them pending; say which, and make the failure/hold update atomic with the next admission it is meant to block. No retroactive promise about an already admitted publication. Add witnesses for a pass under another configuration/rules generation on the same/later commit and for failure racing the next admission. The deployment-specific environment/health contract is correctly left open and need not be designed here.\n\nF2 — Medium, Audits lines127–138, Reuse lines152–175 and retry witness: state how audit execution interacts with reuse. Every:1 is said to run the room audit on every applicable landing, save no run, and still run after cancel/retry. But the audit target is a normal room/landing check, and nothing prevents reuse:true. Its admitted pass from a cancelled reservation on the same tree/config/source/stage meets the listed reuse rule, so the retry can count it without a new room run. Both statements cannot govern the same job. Either require a fresh audit execution (and reject/override reuse for the audit target in this first slice), or explicitly permit cached comparison and change the cost/retry claims and witnesses. Retain the distinction between comparing recorded results and executing a new run. A small rule and one audit+reuse retry witness suffice.\n\nArchitecture: source/reuse/stage and versioned adoption remain sound. The destination is now the right owner of holds; finish its recovery identity and linearization boundary. The reuse index and separation of disabling cache from allowing a fresh fenced run answer original F3 at design level. In the later implementation, compare admitted pass/fail outcomes under the same eligibility rule before requiring a pass for actual reuse; do not compare only successful candidates.\n\nSecurity: original F1's sampling claim is closed by every:1 with future independent randomness/retry identity explicitly open. Outside own-answer/read is correctly an owner-defined observation, not provider truth. Original F4's requester/agent reopen and role citations are corrected. Hold source/generation matching in F1 is still required; recorded timestamps/IDs alone are not that check.\n\nSimplification: keep the existing three concepts, open dependency table and Hugh's four decisions. This requests two short policy rules and discriminating witnesses, not a UI/API schema or implementation commission. No objection to the first production adapter requirement.\n\nValidation: full succe